      "worked": "When $k=-1$, the expression is $-4x-2$. At $x=-1$ it equals $2>0$, so this case fails.\n\nFor the quadratic to be strictly negative for every real $x$, it must open downwards and have no $x$-intercepts. Thus $k+1<0$ and $\\Delta<0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}k+1&<0\\\\k&<-1\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(-4)^2-4(k+1)(k-1)\\\\\\Delta&=20-4k^2\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe discriminant condition gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}20-4k^2&<0\\\\k^2-5&>0\\end{align*}$$\n\nSketch $y=k^2-5$. It opens upwards and crosses the horizontal axis at $k=-\\sqrt{5}$ and $k=\\sqrt{5}$. The graph is above the axis when $k<-\\sqrt{5}$ or $k>\\sqrt{5}$.\n\nCombining this with $k<-1$ gives $\\boxed{k<-\\sqrt{5}}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": [
